---
import Shot from "../../../components/Shot.astro";
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout
  docKey="batch"
  title="Batch"
  description="Stack many folders in one Deptheos run: one stack per folder or auto sort by capture time, exports as it goes, failed stacks, and sequences."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Batch</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A batch stacks many focus brackets in one run, one after another, with the same method and settings.
      Use it after a session in the field or on the rail: point it at the folders, or at one folder holding
      several brackets, and come back to a render of each.
    </p>
    <p>
      Set the method and settings in the inspector first, ideally after stacking one of the brackets by hand to
      check them: every stack in the run uses the settings as they are when it starts. Then choose File ▸ New
      Batch… (⇧⌘B), or <em>New Batch…</em> in the Library's title bar. Or drag two or more folders onto the
      Library and drop them on <em>A Stack per Folder (Batch…)</em>: the dialog opens with them listed. Dropped
      on <em>One Stack</em>, their frames go into one stack instead.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section id="folders">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Folders</h2>
      <p>The top of the dialog lists what the run will read.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><em>Add Folders…</em> adds folders, one or several at a time.</li>
        <li><em>Add Subfolders of…</em> adds every folder inside the one you pick, leaving out its own frames: the usual choice for a day's folders under one parent.</li>
        <li><em>Add the Frames List</em> adds the frames included in the Stacking screen's Frames list, as one more source.</li>
        <li><em>Include deeper folders</em> also reads folders inside those folders.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>A folder added twice is stacked once.</p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot"><Shot name="batch-dialog" width="2880" height="1800" loading="lazy" alt="The Batch dialog with folder list, capture-time auto sort, and export options." /><figcaption>The Batch dialog, ready to start.</figcaption></figure>
  </section>

  <section id="split">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>How to split</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>One stack per folder</strong> makes one render of all the frames in each folder. It suits
        brackets you already keep in folders of their own.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Auto sort: split each folder into stacks by capture time</strong> is for a card's worth of
        brackets in one folder. It reads when each shot was taken and cuts between two shots when the pause is
        both longer than 2 seconds and four times the typical pause of the shots around it, or when the camera
        changes. A slow rail stack with a frame every 5 seconds is not cut at its own pace, and two brackets
        shot a few seconds apart still come apart. Auto sort never mixes frames from two folders. It skips:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>runs of fewer than 5 shots, such as test shots and brackets you abandoned;</li>
        <li>the odd ones out of a run that mixes RAW and other files;</li>
        <li>a folder with too few frames to stack.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The summary at the bottom of the dialog counts the stacks it found and what it skipped before you
        start, so check it. A file with no capture time is placed by the time it was last changed.
      </p>
      <h3>The other choices</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><em>RAW + JPEG pairs</em>: <em>Use RAW</em> or <em>Use JPEG</em>, for shots saved both ways.</li>
        <li>
          <em>Frame order</em>: <em>Name</em>, or <em>Capture time</em>. Auto sort uses capture time only to
          split; inside each stack the frames go in name order unless you choose Capture time, since some
          cameras get capture times wrong by a second or two.
        </li>
        <li><em>RAW decoder</em>, shown when Adobe DNG Converter is installed as well as LibRaw (see <a href="/docs/raw/">RAW, DNG and color</a>).</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="export">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>After stacking: export each render</h2>
      <p>
        Every stack of a batch is kept as a render in the Library, as a stack made by hand is. Tick
        <em>Export each render</em> to also write a file of each one as soon as it is made: choose the format
        (with the JPEG quality, or <em>16 bits per channel</em> for TIFF and PNG) and a folder with <em>Choose Folder…</em>.
        Each file is named after its stack. DNG applies to the RAW stacks; any others in the run are written
        as TIFF. <a href="/docs/export/">Exporting</a> explains the formats.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="running">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>While it runs</h2>
      <p>
        <em>Start Batch</em> runs the stacks one at a time. The Library shows where the run is ("Batch 2 of 5 · bee")
        with a <em>Cancel</em> button. One thing runs at a time, so while a batch runs nothing else can be
        stacked and the list and settings are locked.
      </p>
      <p>
        Cancel (⌘.) stops the stack in progress. Every render already made stays, and the exports already under way
        are finished. When the run ends, a notice sums it up: "Batch: 12 of 14 made · 2 failed", with the
        exports and anything skipped.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="failed">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Stacks that failed</h2>
      <p>
        A stack that fails doesn't stop the run. It is kept under the batch's section in the Library, marked
        <em>Failed</em>, and <em>Failed</em> in the Library's left bar lists every one. Select it to see
        why, then <em>Open to Retry</em>: its frames and settings load into Stacking, to fix (exclude a bad
        frame, try the grid aligner) and Stack again. <em>Delete All Failed Stacks</em> clears them once you are done.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="sequences">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Turntable and grow sequences</h2>
      <p>
        These make an animation rather than a still, as a folder of numbered frames for a video editor such as
        DaVinci Resolve to import as one clip.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Grow sequence</strong>: frame <em>k</em> is the stack of <em>k</em> of the bracket's frames,
          so the subject comes into focus as it plays, from the back (the default) or from the front. The last
          frame is the render itself. It is made from a Pyramid
          render: File ▸ Export Grow Sequence…, or the same item on a render's right-click menu. Retouching is
          not included.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Turntable sequence</strong>: for a subject on a turntable, shot as one bracket per angle, each
          in its own folder. In the Batch dialog, choose One stack per folder and tick <em>Turntable
          sequence</em>. <em>Full</em> turns the finished stacks; <em>Grow across stacks</em> turns and comes
          into focus at once (Pyramid only); <em>Both</em> writes the two.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>The command line makes both too: see <a href="/docs/cli/#sequence">The command line</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
